Title:
HookChain: A Deep Dive into Advanced EDR Bypass Techniques

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains “HookChain,” a user-mode EDR bypass approach that avoids common NTDLL API hooks by chaining indirect syscalls with import table redirection.  
- It targets the problem of EDRs intercepting sensitive Windows APIs (e.g., `NtAllocateVirtualMemory`) via inline hooks in `ntdll.dll`, which can reveal or block injection behavior.  
- The technique dynamically maps System Service Numbers (SSNs), detects hooked syscall stubs by byte-scanning, and uses Halo’s Gate-style neighbor function discovery to execute syscalls from unhooked stubs.  
- HookChain also modifies Import Address Tables (IAT) in modules like `kernel32.dll`/`kernelbase.dll` so higher-level API calls transparently route into the bypass handler.  
- It’s primarily useful for red teamers, pentesters, and malware developers building injectors or post-exploitation tooling in EDR-heavy environments, and it’s interesting because it combines syscall indirection with pervasive API redirection to reduce reliance on a single bypass primitive.

Technical Focus:
- NTDLL inline hook detection via prologue byte scanning
- Indirect syscalls (Halo’s Gate / neighbor syscall stub discovery)
- Dynamic SSN (System Service Number) resolution and syscall list building
- Import Address Table (IAT) patching in `kernel32.dll` / `kernelbase.dll`
- EDR user-mode monitoring model and syscall interception gaps
- Injection workflow integration (shellcode injection; mentions hollowing/reflective loading extensions)

Use Cases:
- Building shellcode injectors that evade user-mode EDR hooks
- Post-exploitation implants that need stealthy memory allocation/protection changes
- Researching robustness of syscall-based evasion across Windows versions (SSN drift)
- Testing/validating EDR coverage around NTDLL hooking and subsystem-layer visibility
- Developing defensive detections for IAT tampering and anomalous syscall stub usage

Keywords:
HookChain, EDR bypass, NTDLL, inline hooks, syscall, SSDT, SSN mapping, indirect syscalls, Halo’s Gate, Heaven’s Gate, SysWhispers, IAT patching, kernel32.dll, kernelbase.dll, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtReadFile, NtWriteFile, shellcode injection, process hollowing, reflective DLL injection, CrowdStrike, SentinelOne